<h2>K-Pop in Seoul — The Pilgrimage</h2>
<p>For K-pop fans, visiting Seoul is a dream. This is where it all happens — where your favorite idols trained, debuted, and perform. The city is saturated with K-pop culture in the most wonderful way. Entertainment company headquarters, idol-themed cafes, official merch stores, music show recordings, and the chance to spot your favorite artists in real life. Here's how to make the most of it.</p>

<h2>🏢 Entertainment Company Headquarters</h2>

<h3>HYBE (하이브) — Yongsan</h3>
<p>Home of BTS, SEVENTEEN, TXT, NewJeans, and more. The new HYBE headquarters in Yongsan opened in 2021 and is a stunning architectural landmark. The Weverse Square area outside has become a fan gathering spot. The HYBE Insight museum inside the building offers an immersive look at HYBE artists — tickets required, book online.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>HYBE Insight:</strong> ₩22,000. Book at weverseshop.io</li>
  <li><strong>Location:</strong> Yongsan Station, 5-minute walk</li>
  <li><strong>Fan tip:</strong> Artists sometimes enter/exit via a separate entrance — fan sites know the schedule</li>
</ul>

<h3>SM Entertainment — Cheongdam</h3>
<p>Home of EXO, NCT, aespa, Red Velvet, SHINee. The SM building in Cheongdam has an official store (SM Store) with official merch. The area around Cheongdam and nearby Apgujeong has many artist-associated cafes and restaurants. SM's Artists Building is where recording happens.</p>

<h3>JYP Entertainment — Cheongdam</h3>
<p>Home of Stray Kids, TWICE, ITZY, DAY6. Located near SM in the Cheongdam area. Official JYP Store nearby. Park Jinyoung's restaurants are in the area.</p>

<h3>YG Entertainment — Mapo-gu</h3>
<p>Home of BLACKPINK, BIGBANG, WINNER, iKON. Located in Hapjeong/Mapo area near the Han River. YGX studio nearby.</p>

<div class="tip-box">
  💡 <strong>Important tip:</strong> Do NOT camp outside entertainment buildings trying to see artists. This is considered stalking behavior, can get you removed by security, and reflects poorly on fandom culture. Artists have strict schedules and separate entrances precisely to avoid this. Respect boundaries — it protects both fans and artists.
</div>

<h2>📺 Music Show Ticket Guide</h2>
<p>Attending a live music show recording is one of the best K-pop experiences in Seoul. Shows are mostly free but require pre-registration.</p>

<h3>Major Music Shows</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Inkigayo (인기가요):</strong> SBS, Sunday recordings. Apply via SBS website 1–2 weeks in advance.</li>
  <li><strong>Music Bank (뮤직뱅크):</strong> KBS2, Friday recordings. Apply at kbs.co.kr</li>
  <li><strong>Show! Music Core (쇼! 음악중심):</strong> MBC, Saturday. Apply at imbc.com</li>
  <li><strong>M Countdown (엠카운트다운):</strong> Mnet, Thursday. Apply via Mnet website.</li>
</ul>
<p>Applications typically open 1–2 weeks before recording. Demand is extremely high for popular groups' comeback weeks. Fan cafes and fan unions often organize group applications — joining an official fan union before your trip significantly improves your chances.</p>

<h2>🛍️ Where to Buy Official Merch</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Weverse Shop (online + offline):</strong> Official global fansite for HYBE artists</li>
  <li><strong>SM Store (Cheongdam):</strong> Official SM Entertainment merch</li>
  <li><strong>JYP Store:</strong> Official JYP merch</li>
  <li><strong>Withdrama (with:D):</strong> Official music show fan sign merchandise</li>
  <li><strong>Music Korea (Myeongdong):</strong> Multi-brand K-pop merchandise, albums, photocards</li>
  <li><strong>Hottracks (Coex, Sinchon):</strong> Albums, merch, lightsticks</li>
</ul>

<div class="tip-box">
  💡 <strong>Photocard trading:</strong> Korea has a huge culture of trading K-pop photocards (individual member cards included in album packaging). There are dedicated photocard trading markets in Hongdae and online communities. If you collect photocards, bring duplicates to trade — other fans are friendly and organized about it.
</div>

<h2>☕ Idol-Themed Cafes (아이돌 카페)</h2>
<p>Idol cafes are temporary pop-up cafes where fan clubs rent a cafe space to celebrate their favorite idol's birthday or comeback. The cafe is decorated with photos and art of the idol, and often has exclusive merchandise. They're called "birthday cafes" or "support cafes."</p>
<ul>
  <li>Check Twitter/X with hashtags like #생카 (birthday cafe) + artist name</li>
  <li>Concentrated in Hongdae, Sinchon, and Seongsu</li>
  <li>Usually open for 1–2 weeks around the idol's birthday</li>
  <li>Drinks are standard cafe fare — you're paying for the experience and exclusive goods</li>
  <li>Fan sites like Naver Cafe or Twitter announce these events</li>
</ul>

<h2>🎤 Fan Signing Events (팬사인회)</h2>
<p>Fan sign events (fansaeng) are small-group events where fans can meet and interact directly with artists. They're incredibly competitive — usually won through album purchase lottery systems.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Buy multiple copies of the album from participating retailers (often Synnara, Hottracks, or artist-specific stores)</li>
  <li>Each copy gives one lottery chance</li>
  <li>Winners are notified by the entertainment company</li>
  <li>Offline fansigns are rare now — most are online video calls (화상통화 팬사인회)</li>
</ul>

<h2>📍 K-Pop Neighborhoods</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cheongdam (청담):</strong> Entertainment company territory. SM, JYP, YG all nearby. High-end, celebrity-sighting neighborhood.</li>
  <li><strong>Hongdae (홍대):</strong> Idol birthday cafes, busking, indie music scene. More accessible.</li>
  <li><strong>Myeongdong:</strong> K-pop merch stores, album shops. Tourist-friendly.</li>
  <li><strong>Sinchon (신촌):</strong> More birthday cafes, fan communities. Near university areas.</li>
</ul>

<h2>🎶 K-Pop Related Experiences</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Norebang (노래방):</strong> Private karaoke rooms. Sing K-pop for hours. ₩15,000–₩30,000/hour for a room. Essential Seoul experience.</li>
  <li><strong>Dance classes:</strong> Learn K-pop choreography at Hongdae studios. 1-hour classes from ₩30,000.</li>
  <li><strong>Music streaming bus tours:</strong> Guided tours of K-pop filming locations, entertainment buildings, and fan spots.</li>
</ul>

